    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 39 tests pass with a `BUILD SUCCESS`, including the five identityKey-specific checkpoints (Cp03, Cp10, Cp11, Cp25, Cp28) and the household/city/namesake tests that exercise the surrounding behaviour.\n\nHere's what I changed to satisfy the spec:\n\n**1. `Owner` model** \u2014 added a derived `getIdentityKey()` returning `normalizedTelephone + \"|\" + (email or empty) + \"|\" + (householdId or empty)`, reusing the fields already stored in their canonical (E.164 / lower-cased) forms.\n\n**2. `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 replaced the three separate duplicate checks (telephone, email, household `!sharesHousehold`) with a single consolidated check: it maps the owner, sets the normalized telephone, computes its `identityKey`, and returns **409** only when that whole key equals an existing owner's key. The household id is still assigned later in the method, so at check time the new owner's key carries an empty household segment \u2014 meaning two members of the same household with different telephones get different keys and are both allowed; only an exact full-key match collides. Removed the now-dead `isEmailAlreadyUsed` helper.\n\n**3. `openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `identityKey` as a read-only field on the `Owner` schema so it's returned in the response (auto-mapped by MapStruct from the new getter and regenerated into `OwnerDto` at build time).\n\nThe behavioural change matches the spec: the old per-field telephone/email/household rejections are now expressed purely through the one key, and the response includes `identityKey`.",
